  slides:
  - subtopicID: 0101
    slideType: 0
    content: "Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) \n\n\tis a system designed to
      prevent workplace illnesses and injuries. It focuses on identifying hazards
      and assessing the risks they may cause. Everyone in the computer and technology
      industries is responsible for maintaining a safe workplace. Hazards should
      be eliminated or modified to reduce the risks they present. This module provides
      guidelines to help users and technicians understand health and safety responsibilities
      in the computer industry."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0101
    slideType: 0
    content: "There are three steps used to manage health and safety at work:\n\r\n\t1.
      Spot the Hazard (Hazard Identification)\n\r\n\t2. Assess the Risk (Risk Assessment)\n\r\n\t3.
      Make the Changes (Risk Control)\n\r\n\tAt work you can use these three Think
      Safe steps to help prevent\r\naccidents. Using the Think Safe Steps."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 0
    content: "Common Hazards\n\n\tA hazard is anything that could hurt you or someone
      else.\n\t\n\tExamples of workplace hazards include:\n\r\n\t\u2022 frayed electrical
      cords (could result in electrical shock)\n\r\n\t\u2022 boxes stacked precariously
      (they could fall on someone)\n\r\n\t\u2022 noisy machinery (could result in
      damage to your hearing)\r\n\n\tDuring work experience, you must remain alert
      to anything that may be dangerous. If you see, hear or smell anything odd,
      take note. If you think it could be a hazard, tell someone.\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 1
    content: "Common Hazards\n\n\tA hazard is anything that could hurt you or someone
      else.\r\n\n\tDuring work experience, you must remain alert to anything that
      may be dangerous. If you see, hear or smell anything odd, take note. If you
      think it could be a hazard, tell someone.\t"
    image: {fileID: -1379164851, guid: 26accc8416e9f68419becb7f4528ad39, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 0
    content: "\tAssessing the risk means working out how likely it is that a hazard
      will harm someone and how serious the harm could be. Whenever you spot a hazard,
      assess the risk by asking yourself two questions:\r\n\n\t\u2022 How likely
      is it that the hazard could harm me or someone else?\n\r\n\t\u2022 How badly
      could I or someone else be harmed?\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "Physical Hazards\n\n\tComputer technicians should be aware of hazards
      such as cables across floors, unstable equipment, and tools left on ladders.
      Temporary cables should be marked with warning signs or secured with gaffers
      tape. Permanent cables should be organized using cable ties, wraps, or raceways.
      Proper organization and secure equipment placement help prevent workplace accidents
      and injuries.\t"
    image: {fileID: 397530054, guid: 0165cc06b85e5a34bb2d324f851d700b, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "Mechanical Hazards\n\n\tWhen working with electronic equipment, always
      consider how it could cause injury. Be careful of moving parts, hot components,
      and sharp edges that can cause serious harm. Staying alert and handling equipment
      carefully can help prevent accidents and injuries.\t"
    image: {fileID: 852546510, guid: 2eb36148423c36049a41bad6c14f2806, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "Chemical Hazards\n\n\tChemicals used for electronic equipment can be
      harmful if swallowed or if they contact the skin or eyes. Always read and follow
      the warnings and instructions on chemical labels before use. Be especially
      careful when handling printer ink and toner, as they can stain skin, clothing,
      and surfaces.\t"
    image: {fileID: 221726296, guid: 42e4763059ae8ca46aa4ee77249e7263, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "Electric Shock Hazard\n\n\tComputers contain different voltages, but
      the power supply can have a dangerous 220 volts. Always unplug equipment before
      working inside it whenever possible. If you must work on powered equipment,
      remove jewelry and wristwatches to reduce the risk of electrical injury."
    image: {fileID: -813718166, guid: 8578f1bc93ea4cd48a5520dd7c525dd8, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "CRT Monitor High-Voltage Hazard\n\n\tCRT monitors can be extremely
      dangerous because they contain high-voltage components that may hold a charge
      even after being unplugged. A CRT\u2019s anode can carry up to 25,000 volts,
      so repairs should be handled by qualified professionals whenever possible.
      If opening one is unavoidable, proper discharge procedures must be followed
      to prevent electrical shock."
    image: {fileID: -1760798639, guid: a15350d8dff77ae4bbc9faea882c0b2e, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0104
    slideType: 0
    content: "Occupational Health and Safety Standards\n\n\tEveryone has a responsibility
      to report and address workplace hazards to protect others.\n\nProcedure:\n\n\t\u2022
      Identify the hazard.\n\n\t\u2022 Clear the area around the hazard.\n\n\t\u2022
      Partition or clearly mark the hazard area.\n\n\t\u2022 Safely clear the hazard
      if possible.\n\n\t\u2022 If it cannot be safely cleared, report it to the appropriate
      person.\n\n\t\u2022 Complete the proper documentation after the hazard is cleared.\n\nAll
      hazards should be reported using an Accident Report Form to help prevent future
      incidents."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0105
    slideType: 0
    content: "Accident Reports\n\t\n\tForms are used to give specific details with
      regards to the accidents happened in the laboratory during practical activities.
      Accident reports contain the following details:\n\r\n\t\u2022 Name of the person
      injured\n\r\n\t\u2022 Date and time of the accident\n\r\n\t\u2022 Type of injury\n\r\n\t\u2022
      First aid given\n\r\n\t\u2022 Action taken to prevent further accidents\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0106
    slideType: 1
    content: "FIre Exits\n\t\n\tAll fire exits must be kept clear of obstacles such
      as chairs and boxes. Everyone is responsible for keeping fire exit doorways
      and nearby areas unobstructed. Corridors should have equipment stored on only
      one side to ensure a clear path during emergencies.\t"
    image: {fileID: -1422242266, guid: c2de2cdf689aeb8468030add18e16159, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 1
    content: "Personal Safety While Working with PC\u2019s\n\t\n\tComputer equipment
      can cause serious injury or even death if proper safety guidelines are not
      followed. Before working with any computer equipment, technicians should take
      the necessary precautions to ensure their safety and the safety of others.\t"
    image: {fileID: 1060451888, guid: 6a567d5d88d6fc84bba860d7f3334f10, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 0
    content: "\tThe following are some precautionary measures to take before working
      with any computer equipment:\n\r\n\t\u2022 Always ground yourself before touching
      any part of the computer\n\r\n\t\u2022 Do not work alone\n\r\n\t\u2022 Be careful
      with tools that may cause short circuit\n\r\n\t\u2022 Replace only fuses with
      those proper ratings\n\r\n\t\u2022 Wear safety glasses for protection against
      sparks and metal fragmented\n\r\n\t\u2022 Use only grounded plugs and receptacles\n\r\n\t\u2022
      Working area should have ventilations, trash can, fire exit and capable of
      being disinfect.\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 0
    content: "\t\u2022 Keep one hand in your pocket when working live circuit.\n\r\n\t\u2022
      Wear rubber sole shoes when standing on the ground or in a concrete floor.\n\r\n\t\u2022
      Always power off and unplug the computer before working on it.\n\r\n\t\u2022
      Wear hard hat when someone working above you\n\r\n\t\u2022 When making circuit
      changes, switch off and unplug the power cord from the equipment then discharge
      the capacitors."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "Anti-Static and Safety Precautions\n\n\tStatic electricity is a small
      electrical discharge that can occur when two objects have different electrical
      potentials. Although people may not feel static shocks until they reach several
      thousand volts, even less than 30 volts can damage sensitive computer components.
      Computer technicians must therefore protect equipment from static electricity
      to prevent damage. They should also take proper precautions to avoid electrical
      injury."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "Safety and Anti-Static Rules\n\r\n\t\u2022 When possible, try to avoid
      working in carpeted areas. Carpeting greatly increases static buildup within
      your body.\n\r\n\t\u2022 Always use an anti-static wrist strap when working
      on a computer (except when working on monitors)\n\r\n\t\u2022 Always disconnect
      a computer from the AC power and from any powered peripherals while you are
      working on it.\n\r\n\t\u2022 Always grasp a metal part of the computer chassis
      with your bare hand before you touch anything inside. Do this even if you are
      wearing an anti-static wristband."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "\t\u2022 Always handle electronic components by a non-conducting (nonmetallic)
      edge. Don't touch the pins or other connectors.\r\n\t\n\t\u2022 Never plug
      an ATX power supply into AC power unless it is connected either to a computer's
      motherboard or to a dummy test load.\r\n\t\n\t\u2022 Always use a UL-approved
      surge protector or an Uninterruptible Power Supply that incorporates surge
      and spike protection.\r\n\t\n\t\u2022 Never eat, drink, or smoke while working
      on a computer."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "The 5 'S' Process: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke\n\n\tThe
      5S Process is a workplace organization system focused on cleanliness, efficiency,
      safety, and standardization. It was developed in Japan and is based on five
      Japanese terms: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, and Shitsuke. In English, these
      are commonly represented as Sort, Set in Place, Shine, Standardize, and Sustain.
      A well-organized workplace can improve safety, productivity, worker morale,
      and responsibility.\n\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "Seiri\n\n\tSeiri (Sort) is the first step of the 5S process, which
      involves removing unwanted, unnecessary, and unrelated items from the workplace.
      Only materials needed for work should remain, and even necessary items should
      be kept to a minimum. This helps simplify tasks, make better use of space,
      and avoid unnecessary purchases.\n\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "Seiton\n\n\tSeiton (Set in Place) focuses on organizing items for efficiency.
      Every tool, material, and item should have an assigned place where it can be
      quickly accessed and returned. Proper organization improves workflow and worker
      productivity. Each location should also be clearly labeled for easy identification.\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "Seiso\n\n\tSeiso (Shine) is the third step of 5S, which focuses on
      keeping the workplace clean. Everyone in the organization, from workers to
      managers, is responsible for cleaning their assigned areas. No area should
      be left uncleaned, and the workplace should always be kept presentable."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "Seiketsu\n\n\tSeiketsu (Standardize) is the fourth step of 5S, which
      focuses on creating standards for maintaining cleanliness and order. It includes
      keeping both the workplace and personnel clean and organized. Visual management,
      such as color-coding, helps identify problems or abnormalities easily. Workers
      should be trained to recognize and correct these issues immediately.\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "Shitsuke\n\n\tShitsuke (Sustain/Discipline) is the fifth and final
      step of 5S, which focuses on maintaining orderliness and practicing the first
      four S consistently. It involves eliminating bad habits and developing good
      ones through regular practice. The goal is for workers to maintain cleanliness
      and organization voluntarily, without constant reminders from management."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0110
    slideType: 0
    content: "Make Yourself a Desktop Cleaner\n\n\tHow to manage your desktop:\n\n1)
      Create 5 folders in My Documents called:\n\n\t\u2022 Inbox \u2013 Store unprocessed
      files that do not have a specific place yet, such as downloads and files from
      colleagues.\n\n\t\u2022 Action Items \u2013 Store files that require actions
      taking more than 2 minutes, such as forms and files to upload.\n\n\t\u2022
      On Hold \u2013 Store items you are not ready to work on yet, such as articles,
      project ideas, and class information.\n\n\t\u2022 Current Projects \u2013 Store
      files related to active projects, with a separate folder for each project.\n\n\t\u2022
      Archive \u2013 Store completed projects, reference materials, and files you
      may need again.\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0110
    slideType: 0
    content: 'Tip: Adding an underscore (_) before a folder name makes it appear
      at the top of the list in My Documents.


      2) Move all relevant files
      from your desktop into these folders. If you have many files, move them all
      into the Inbox first and organize them later.


      3) Keep only program
      icons on the desktop to maintain a clean and organized workspace.'
    image: {fileID: 0}
